      <h1>Market Data Methodology</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Market Intelligence · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Every figure published here carries a <strong>source</strong> and a
        <strong>date</strong>. Numbers are collected from primary sources first, cross-checked, and flagged rather than
        silently averaged when sources conflict. No figure is fabricated or extrapolated without saying so.
      </div>

      <h2>Source hierarchy</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Source type</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1 — Primary</td><td>Government &amp; official statistics</td><td>Customs/export data, national statistics bureaus, trade bodies</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2 — Industry</td><td>Established industry publications</td><td>Trade associations, reputable market research</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3 — Secondary</td><td>News &amp; aggregators</td><td>Used for leads, not final numbers</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The rules</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Source and date on every figure</strong> — a number without both is not published.</li>
        <li><strong>Conflict is flagged</strong> — when two sources disagree, the disagreement is stated and its likely reason noted, not hidden behind an average.</li>
        <li><strong>No fabricated market size, price or growth rate</strong> — if a figure cannot be sourced, it is omitted.</li>
        <li><strong>Unit and currency are stated</strong> — no silent conversion or false precision.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Position:</strong> Market data is only as trustworthy as its sourcing — a chart with no source is
        decoration, and a sourced number with a conflict you can see is the honest version.</p>
        <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> Battery-market figures vary wildly between sources because of different
        definitions and dates. The disciplined move is to show the source and the conflict, not to produce a fake-clean
        single number.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not investment advice.</em></p>
      </div>

